obile" class=3D"tnc-gallery--caption photo-cap _mobile family-serif fz-v6 f=
w-v3">Ntipiyon Nonguta and her son Bernard, 1, rest in the shade of a tree =
in Ewaso Village, Kenya, in 2015. Nonguta made and sold handicrafts through=
 BeadWORKS. =C2=A9 Ami Vitale</p>
                                            <p id=3D"tnc-gallery--headline"=
 class=3D"_heading family-serif">A Community Partnership Protects a Kenyan =
Landscape</p>
                                            <p id=3D"tnc-gallery--text" cla=
ss=3D"_content family-sans fz-v9 lh-v9">On the wide, open expanses of centr=
al Kenya, wildlife like lions and elephants often encounter people and loca=
l communities in the routines of daily life. To help make space for everyon=
e=E2=80=94and reduce human-wildlife conflict=E2=80=94TNC bought thousands o=
f acres of former private ranchland in this region, transferring it in 2014=
 to the newly created Loisaba Community Trust. The project offered a model =
of a new kind of conservation effort in Kenya: Instead of pushing people of=
f their lands to create game parks for wildlife, the Trust is run with the =
involvement of communities that promote the sustainable management of their=
 lands. Today these 58,000 acres provide room for wildlife to roam and allo=
w for ecotourism that supports schools and community health programs and cr=
eates new local livelihoods through efforts like BeadWORKS, in which women =
like Nonguta generate additional income by making and selling handicrafts.<=
/p>
